00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:40.140
Second story is Ukraine's distributed solar-keeping lights on while the grid gets hit.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:52.960
As Russia attacks Ukraine's grid, solar offers a path to resilience. Distributed solar installations have kept power flowing to hospitals, schools, and homes, even when centralized grid infrastructure was struck.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:59.620
Ukrainian communities and businesses have added panels and batteries at a pace that outstrips pre-war plans.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:07.000
The modular nature of rooftop and ground mount systems means damage to one site does not collapse service for an entire region.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:14.020
Early data show these smaller arrays reduce outage duration compared with reliance on the main transmission network alone.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:20.200
Communities and businesses installed the systems faster than planners had projected before the conflict began.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:26.000
Hospitals and schools continued operations during strikes because individual arrays stayed online.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:29.700
The modular design limits the reach of any single attack.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:35.560
Data collected so far indicates shorter average outage times when distributed generation is present.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.020
